1
00:00:25,959 --> 00:00:28,193
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,336
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,483
♪ ♪

4
00:01:17,477 --> 00:01:20,411
♪ ♪

5
00:01:50,277 --> 00:01:53,211
♪ ♪

6
00:02:08,495 --> 00:02:10,829
Laura, do you think
I like working late?

7
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:13,465
Stop lying to me, David!

8
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:14,733
It's that blonde!

9
00:02:14,768 --> 00:02:16,067
Stop lying to me!

10
00:02:16,102 --> 00:02:17,569
You're seeing her again!

11
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:20,038
I told you and I've
told you it's over!

12
00:02:20,073 --> 00:02:21,773
You're going to see her tonight.

13
00:02:21,809 --> 00:02:24,742
Laura, I'm ending this
conversation right now!

14
00:02:28,815 --> 00:02:31,550
Excuse me, but Mr. Freemont's
meeting's just ending.

15
00:02:31,585 --> 00:02:34,145
If you want to catch
him, it's a good time.

16
00:02:52,939 --> 00:02:54,973
I didn't want to bother you.

17
00:02:55,008 --> 00:02:58,610
But when this came
up on the computer,

18
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:01,446
I thought I should mention it.

19
00:03:01,482 --> 00:03:04,148
No, no, you did the right thing.

20
00:03:06,052 --> 00:03:08,612
This is very distressing.

21
00:03:11,191 --> 00:03:13,224
Have you, uh...

22
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:15,104
mentioned this to anyone else?

23
00:03:15,128 --> 00:03:16,495
Do you want me to?

24
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:19,297
Not till we sort it out.

25
00:03:19,332 --> 00:03:21,544
If your computer comes
up with anything else,

26
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:23,935
just bring it to me.

27
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,441
What was that about?

28
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:31,577
Oh, nothing.

29
00:03:31,612 --> 00:03:35,814
A very old, very
valued customer...

30
00:03:35,849 --> 00:03:38,950
with a very large overdraft.

31
00:03:38,985 --> 00:03:41,152
Well, this valued customer

32
00:03:41,187 --> 00:03:43,588
certainly doesn't
have that problem.

33
00:04:23,497 --> 00:04:25,242
The man who just came in here...

34
00:04:25,266 --> 00:04:26,309
where did he go?

35
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:28,567
Sorry, ma'am... I
can't tell you that.

36
00:04:31,705 --> 00:04:32,938
Where is he?

37
00:04:37,111 --> 00:04:40,545
He went up to the penthouse.

38
00:04:40,581 --> 00:04:42,614
You'll need my
key for the elevator.

39
00:04:44,417 --> 00:04:45,951
What are you doing? I'm reaching

40
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:47,919
for the key.

41
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:51,267
Hurry up.

42
00:04:51,291 --> 00:04:53,358
Easy, lady, easy.

43
00:05:04,071 --> 00:05:05,136
This couldn't wait.

44
00:05:05,172 --> 00:05:07,172
I had to see you.

45
00:05:16,149 --> 00:05:17,149
Penthouse.

46
00:05:20,587 --> 00:05:22,754
David.

47
00:05:30,263 --> 00:05:31,340
Hey, Julie.

48
00:05:31,364 --> 00:05:32,564
Hi... hi, Gus.

49
00:05:32,599 --> 00:05:33,799
New suit.

50
00:05:33,834 --> 00:05:35,767
Do you like it? Ooh, yeah.

51
00:05:35,803 --> 00:05:37,435
You don't think it's too...

52
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:40,172
No, it's right on the money.

53
00:05:40,207 --> 00:05:41,718
Thank you. Yeah.

54
00:05:41,742 --> 00:05:44,576
I understand you've
taken on Laura McCord.

55
00:05:44,611 --> 00:05:45,689
Yeah.

56
00:05:45,713 --> 00:05:48,313
Interesting how
she pointed a gun

57
00:05:48,348 --> 00:05:50,782
at a security
guard, and then, uh,

58
00:05:50,818 --> 00:05:53,618
turned up next to the
body of her dead husband

59
00:05:53,653 --> 00:05:56,654
with the gun in her hand.

60
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:58,857
She's admitted to all that.

61
00:05:58,892 --> 00:06:00,058
Then, how can you

62
00:06:00,093 --> 00:06:01,859
possibly think she's innocent?

63
00:06:01,895 --> 00:06:03,939
There was a witness, a woman.

64
00:06:03,963 --> 00:06:05,942
If I can find that witness...

65
00:06:05,966 --> 00:06:08,300
Have you read
the residue report?

66
00:06:08,335 --> 00:06:10,234
Well, sure.

67
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:14,672
I don't guess you would
spring for a bottle of pop.

68
00:06:14,707 --> 00:06:16,007
Sure.

69
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:17,308
What do you want?

70
00:06:17,343 --> 00:06:18,944
Grape.

71
00:06:20,713 --> 00:06:22,291
Grape. Orange.

72
00:06:22,315 --> 00:06:23,481
Orange. Fine.

73
00:06:23,516 --> 00:06:25,183
Orange.

74
00:06:26,753 --> 00:06:28,993
Thanks. Thank you.

75
00:06:31,191 --> 00:06:33,791
The residue report
shows she had powder

76
00:06:33,827 --> 00:06:35,560
all over her hand,

77
00:06:35,596 --> 00:06:37,662
so she obviously fired the gun

78
00:06:37,698 --> 00:06:38,963
and killed her husband.

79
00:06:38,998 --> 00:06:41,799
Well, there were two
shots several seconds apart.

80
00:06:41,835 --> 00:06:43,601
She shot him twice.

81
00:06:43,636 --> 00:06:45,281
That doesn't make any sense.

82
00:06:45,305 --> 00:06:48,406
The coroner's report said
that each shot was fatal.

83
00:06:48,442 --> 00:06:49,774
Why shoot him twice?

84
00:06:49,809 --> 00:06:51,376
Doesn't stand to reason.

85
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:55,012
Oh, come on, we're
not talking about reason.

86
00:06:55,048 --> 00:06:56,192
We're not? No.

87
00:06:56,216 --> 00:06:57,515
We're talking...

88
00:06:57,551 --> 00:07:01,619
"Hell hath no fury
like a woman scorned."

89
00:07:01,654 --> 00:07:02,854
Oh.

90
00:07:02,889 --> 00:07:04,989
Who doesn't know that?

91
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:07,792
Laura McCord is
jealous, excitable,

92
00:07:07,827 --> 00:07:10,172
high-strung, and she
can hold a grudge,

93
00:07:10,196 --> 00:07:12,764
but she did not
kill her husband.

94
00:07:12,799 --> 00:07:15,767
Somebody besides David
McCord and the witness

95
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:18,903
was in that apartment
when Laura arrived.

96
00:07:18,939 --> 00:07:21,673
That person killed David McCord.

97
00:07:21,708 --> 00:07:23,619
And when Laura
collapsed, he put the gun

98
00:07:23,643 --> 00:07:25,821
in her hand, fired
a second shot.

99
00:07:25,845 --> 00:07:29,013
That is some incredible story.

100
00:07:29,048 --> 00:07:30,159
It is, isn't it?

101
00:07:30,183 --> 00:07:31,149
Yeah.

102
00:07:31,184 --> 00:07:33,017
Well, I don't buy it.

103
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:35,319
Enjoy your lunch.

104
00:07:35,354 --> 00:07:36,498
Oh, I've been trying to.

105
00:07:36,522 --> 00:07:38,322
Oh.

106
00:07:40,426 --> 00:07:43,127
That is a nice suit.

107
00:07:44,964 --> 00:07:47,432
And you are the security guard

108
00:07:47,467 --> 00:07:49,333
at the Carlisle Arms?

109
00:07:49,369 --> 00:07:50,747
For seven years now, yes.

110
00:07:50,771 --> 00:07:53,070
Were you working the night
of David McCord's murder?

111
00:07:53,106 --> 00:07:54,205
I was.

112
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:56,808
Did anything unusual
happen that night?

113
00:07:56,843 --> 00:07:57,853
Yes.

114
00:07:57,877 --> 00:08:00,145
A woman came in waving a gun

115
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:02,291
and demanding to know
where Mr. McCord was.

116
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:03,581
What did you do?

117
00:08:03,616 --> 00:08:05,116
She had a gun on me.

118
00:08:05,152 --> 00:08:06,896
I used my key
to let her upstairs.

119
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:08,186
And then what did you do?

120
00:08:08,221 --> 00:08:09,654
I called the police.

121
00:08:09,689 --> 00:08:11,934
And when the police arrived,
I went outside to meet them.

122
00:08:11,958 --> 00:08:13,191
Uh... we heard a gunshot.

123
00:08:13,226 --> 00:08:15,038
Then, we went up
to the penthouse.

124
00:08:15,062 --> 00:08:18,696
And what did you
find in that penthouse?

125
00:08:18,731 --> 00:08:19,964
The dead man.

126
00:08:19,999 --> 00:08:22,600
And next to him was the
woman from the lobby...

127
00:08:22,635 --> 00:08:24,169
with the gun in her hand.

128
00:08:24,204 --> 00:08:26,203
Can you identify that woman?

129
00:08:26,239 --> 00:08:27,438
Yes, I can.

130
00:08:27,473 --> 00:08:28,740
Is she here in the courtroom?

131
00:08:28,775 --> 00:08:29,741
She is.

132
00:08:29,776 --> 00:08:32,110
Would you point her out to us?

133
00:08:32,145 --> 00:08:33,623
Your Honor!

134
00:08:33,647 --> 00:08:35,246
Your Honor, I want to testify.

135
00:08:35,281 --> 00:08:36,926
That's the woman
I caught with David.

136
00:08:36,950 --> 00:08:38,416
His mistress.

137
00:08:38,451 --> 00:08:39,617
I was there.

138
00:08:39,652 --> 00:08:40,785
I saw the murder.

139
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:42,698
I want to testify.

140
00:08:42,722 --> 00:08:45,282
Order in the court.

141
00:08:45,425 --> 00:08:47,385
Counsel, please
approach the bench.

142
00:08:49,862 --> 00:08:52,597
Uh... What do you think?

143
00:08:52,632 --> 00:08:53,698
I don't know.

144
00:08:53,733 --> 00:08:56,334
Your Honor, the people
would like a recess

145
00:08:56,369 --> 00:08:57,969
so I can interview this woman.

146
00:08:58,004 --> 00:09:00,104
Your Honor, if justice

147
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:01,217
is what we're looking for,

148
00:09:01,241 --> 00:09:02,418
this woman is an eyewitness.

149
00:09:02,442 --> 00:09:04,153
Why don't we put
her on the stand

150
00:09:04,177 --> 00:09:06,144
and hear her story?

151
00:09:06,179 --> 00:09:07,145
Miss March?

152
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:10,382
Just so I understand,
you want to waive

153
00:09:10,417 --> 00:09:12,750
any defense right
to a continuance

154
00:09:12,785 --> 00:09:14,919
and just put this
woman on the stand?

155
00:09:14,954 --> 00:09:17,122
Yeah.

156
00:09:19,059 --> 00:09:20,892
Why not?

157
00:09:22,162 --> 00:09:23,906
I'll give you a few
minutes to prepare.

158
00:09:23,930 --> 00:09:26,064
Thank you, Your Honor.

159
00:09:34,474 --> 00:09:36,552
That woman saw
everything, and she's

160
00:09:36,576 --> 00:09:37,620
willing to testify.

161
00:09:37,644 --> 00:09:40,044
Now, let's hear
what she has to say.

162
00:09:40,079 --> 00:09:43,448
Mr. Matlock, Miss
March, may we proceed?

163
00:09:43,483 --> 00:09:44,793
- Yes, Your Honor.
- Yes, Your Honor.

164
00:09:44,817 --> 00:09:46,985
Bailiff, bring the witness in.

165
00:09:54,527 --> 00:09:56,472
Well, bring her in.

166
00:09:56,496 --> 00:09:58,863
Uh, Your Honor,
she's disappeared.

167
00:10:15,248 --> 00:10:16,948
Julie! Oh.

168
00:10:16,983 --> 00:10:18,861
Ben, you scared
me half to death.

169
00:10:18,885 --> 00:10:20,128
What are you doing here?

170
00:10:20,152 --> 00:10:21,252
Same as you, I guess.

171
00:10:21,287 --> 00:10:22,620
Looking for evidence.

172
00:10:23,723 --> 00:10:25,167
Are you all right?

173
00:10:25,191 --> 00:10:26,524
In a minute.

174
00:10:26,559 --> 00:10:28,003
Court order sealed
that elevator.

175
00:10:28,027 --> 00:10:30,039
I had to walk all the
way up five or six floors.

176
00:10:30,063 --> 00:10:31,529
Seven, actually.

177
00:10:31,564 --> 00:10:33,264
Ooh.

178
00:10:35,402 --> 00:10:38,669
What do you suppose a...
apartment like this costs?

179
00:10:38,705 --> 00:10:39,904
A lot.

180
00:10:41,007 --> 00:10:43,241
Ben, your jacket.

181
00:10:45,678 --> 00:10:48,546
Oh... paint.

182
00:10:48,581 --> 00:10:51,049
Paint on my jacket.

183
00:10:51,084 --> 00:10:52,183
Julie March.

184
00:10:52,218 --> 00:10:54,251
Yes.

185
00:10:54,287 --> 00:10:55,853
Where?

186
00:10:55,889 --> 00:10:57,989
What time was that?

187
00:10:58,024 --> 00:10:59,824
Uh, well, I think

188
00:10:59,859 --> 00:11:01,403
we're going to
need more than that,

189
00:11:01,427 --> 00:11:03,472
but, uh, would you
keep me informed?

190
00:11:03,496 --> 00:11:04,962
Thanks.

191
00:11:06,866 --> 00:11:07,910
Anything?

192
00:11:07,934 --> 00:11:09,466
Uh, I don't know.

193
00:11:09,502 --> 00:11:11,202
The police found a cab driver

194
00:11:11,237 --> 00:11:13,371
who may or may not
have picked her up.

195
00:11:13,406 --> 00:11:15,206
Where did he take her?

196
00:11:15,241 --> 00:11:16,674
The bus station.

197
00:11:16,709 --> 00:11:18,376
No, not our witness.

198
00:11:18,411 --> 00:11:20,490
I don't think the bus
station. Uh-uh, I doubt it.

199
00:11:20,514 --> 00:11:23,080
Uh, why do you think a...

200
00:11:23,116 --> 00:11:26,317
woman like... like that

201
00:11:26,353 --> 00:11:28,631
would have, uh, hiking boots

202
00:11:28,655 --> 00:11:30,099
with, uh, mud all over them?

203
00:11:30,123 --> 00:11:33,991
Well, maybe...
she likes to hike.

204
00:11:34,026 --> 00:11:36,094
I mean, even a woman like that

205
00:11:36,129 --> 00:11:40,165
can't spend all of
her time... like that.

206
00:11:42,702 --> 00:11:43,702
Ah.

207
00:11:44,871 --> 00:11:48,373
Well... my turn to buy lunch.

208
00:11:48,408 --> 00:11:49,941
Oh... another time.

209
00:11:49,976 --> 00:11:51,776
Well... okay.

210
00:11:51,812 --> 00:11:52,777
Busy.

211
00:11:52,813 --> 00:11:55,373
Okay, yeah, oh, yeah.

212
00:12:16,169 --> 00:12:18,869
What are you doing here?

213
00:12:18,905 --> 00:12:20,872
What are<i> you</i> doing here?

214
00:12:20,907 --> 00:12:22,140
Well,

215
00:12:22,175 --> 00:12:24,708
after we left that
woman's apartment,

216
00:12:24,744 --> 00:12:27,044
I got to thinking
about the red clay

217
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:28,879
you found on her shoes.

218
00:12:28,915 --> 00:12:31,916
I got to wondering why a
woman with an expensive car

219
00:12:31,951 --> 00:12:33,829
like that in the garage
would take a taxi.

220
00:12:33,853 --> 00:12:36,688
And I talked to
that taxi driver,

221
00:12:36,723 --> 00:12:39,356
and he told me that the woman
he picked up spoke with a...

222
00:12:39,392 --> 00:12:41,693
Slight hill accent.

223
00:12:41,728 --> 00:12:43,339
And he brought her
to the bus station.

224
00:12:43,363 --> 00:12:45,863
And court reconvenes
in 48 hours,

225
00:12:45,898 --> 00:12:49,100
so I have 48 hours
to find that witness.

226
00:12:49,135 --> 00:12:50,568
Hi.

227
00:12:50,603 --> 00:12:52,915
By any chance, do you recall

228
00:12:52,939 --> 00:12:56,674
a young woman,
early 20s, very pretty,

229
00:12:56,709 --> 00:12:59,277
who might have
bought a ticket, oh, say,

230
00:12:59,312 --> 00:13:02,513
in the last couple of hours
to somewhere in the hills?

231
00:13:02,549 --> 00:13:04,282
No.

232
00:13:04,317 --> 00:13:07,184
Let's put it this way.

233
00:13:07,220 --> 00:13:09,954
Where in the hills
would a person go

234
00:13:09,989 --> 00:13:12,757
if a person went to the hills?

235
00:13:12,792 --> 00:13:14,159
I don't know.

236
00:13:14,194 --> 00:13:16,961
Gunther's Creek.

237
00:13:16,997 --> 00:13:19,296
That's where the mud came
from that was on her boots

238
00:13:19,332 --> 00:13:20,332
and that's where she is.

239
00:13:20,366 --> 00:13:22,567
You did sell a
ticket to a pretty girl

240
00:13:22,602 --> 00:13:24,602
to Gunther's Creek, didn't you?

241
00:13:24,637 --> 00:13:26,070
Yeah.

242
00:13:28,041 --> 00:13:31,009
I would like a
window seat, please.

243
00:13:31,044 --> 00:13:32,844
I'll drive you. What?

244
00:13:32,879 --> 00:13:35,013
We might get lucky.

245
00:13:35,048 --> 00:13:38,149
We might find the witness
before court reconvenes.

246
00:13:41,054 --> 00:13:43,166
Mrs. Morris, your husband is

247
00:13:43,190 --> 00:13:44,955
waiting at Arrival Gate 50.

248
00:13:44,991 --> 00:13:46,257
Right over here.

249
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:56,734
We should do this more often.

250
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:58,447
Chase a witness
back up in the hills?

251
00:13:58,471 --> 00:14:00,048
No.

252
00:14:00,072 --> 00:14:02,707
No, I mean, get
out of our offices.

253
00:14:02,742 --> 00:14:04,553
Yeah. You know, take the time

254
00:14:04,577 --> 00:14:08,212
to stop and smell the flowers.

255
00:14:08,247 --> 00:14:10,014
Yeah. Yeah.

256
00:14:10,049 --> 00:14:12,750
Oh, Ben, I'll get
that paint out for you.

257
00:14:12,785 --> 00:14:14,885
Just give me your
jacket when we get back.

258
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:17,821
Oh, I can't give you
my clothes to clean.

259
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:19,656
You don't take in laundry.

260
00:14:19,692 --> 00:14:22,260
Why not? It's just a jacket.

261
00:14:22,295 --> 00:14:23,527
That's too much trouble.

262
00:14:23,562 --> 00:14:24,828
Oh, not as much trouble

263
00:14:24,864 --> 00:14:27,465
as driving me all the
way to Gunther's Creek.

264
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:29,734
Well, actually, I'm...

265
00:14:29,769 --> 00:14:31,702
I'm doing that more for myself.

266
00:14:31,738 --> 00:14:33,371
See, when we find that witness,

267
00:14:33,406 --> 00:14:36,173
she'll identify David
McCord's killer.

268
00:14:36,208 --> 00:14:38,909
Right. Laura McCord.

269
00:14:38,944 --> 00:14:42,680
Or you mean who<i>
framed</i> Laura McCord.

270
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:52,824
♪ ♪

271
00:15:22,855 --> 00:15:24,055
I'm going to stop and talk

272
00:15:24,090 --> 00:15:25,322
to these boys for awhile.

273
00:15:25,357 --> 00:15:27,436
I'll see what I can
find out in the store.

274
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:29,393
Okay.

275
00:15:36,535 --> 00:15:38,002
Hey, hey, hey!

276
00:15:38,037 --> 00:15:40,071
Good pickin', good pickin'.

277
00:15:40,106 --> 00:15:41,972
Good pickin'. Boy!

278
00:15:43,409 --> 00:15:45,220
Sure gets hot up here in Georgia

279
00:15:45,244 --> 00:15:46,855
this time of year, doesn't it?

280
00:15:48,314 --> 00:15:49,958
Yeah, it does. It gets hot.

281
00:15:49,982 --> 00:15:51,849
Yup, yup, yup.

282
00:15:51,884 --> 00:15:53,084
That's a nice guitar.

283
00:15:53,119 --> 00:15:55,331
I used to, I used
to do a little pickin'.

284
00:15:55,355 --> 00:15:56,565
I used to pick all the time.

285
00:15:56,589 --> 00:15:59,590
I used... I used to pick
with St. Clair Bass Night.

286
00:15:59,625 --> 00:16:02,226
I guess you remember
St. Clair Bass Night?

287
00:16:02,261 --> 00:16:03,739
Went barefooted all the time.

288
00:16:03,763 --> 00:16:05,140
Said shoes bothered his feet.

289
00:16:05,164 --> 00:16:08,032
Yeah. Well, he was...
He was a great picker.

290
00:16:08,068 --> 00:16:09,833
Great picker.

291
00:16:10,937 --> 00:16:13,638
Oh!

292
00:16:17,009 --> 00:16:18,976
♪ Well... ♪

293
00:16:19,011 --> 00:16:20,889
♪ Honey, let me
be your salty dog ♪

294
00:16:20,913 --> 00:16:22,813
♪ Or I won't be
your man at all ♪

295
00:16:22,849 --> 00:16:25,282
♪ Honey, let me be
your salty dog... ♪

296
00:16:26,686 --> 00:16:28,452
Pop the hood for me, boss.

297
00:16:30,022 --> 00:16:32,923
♪ Honey, let me
be your salty dog. ♪

298
00:16:32,958 --> 00:16:34,891
♪ ♪

299
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:02,219
Hey-hey-hey!

300
00:17:02,255 --> 00:17:03,855
All right!

301
00:17:08,828 --> 00:17:11,896
Well, I guess you
can... look at it this way,

302
00:17:11,931 --> 00:17:13,398
I can only get better.

303
00:17:15,334 --> 00:17:17,894
I enjoyed that. I enjoyed that.

304
00:17:18,537 --> 00:17:19,837
Yes, sir!

305
00:17:19,873 --> 00:17:22,640
The Georgia hills are
beautiful this time of year.

306
00:17:22,675 --> 00:17:24,242
Beautiful.

307
00:17:24,277 --> 00:17:27,211
I guess folks born here go away

308
00:17:27,247 --> 00:17:29,546
looking for someplace
that's better,

309
00:17:29,582 --> 00:17:31,749
always wind up coming back here.

310
00:17:31,785 --> 00:17:33,584
Ben? Ben?

311
00:17:33,619 --> 00:17:35,653
Look at this quilt. Ooh...

312
00:17:35,688 --> 00:17:38,289
The workmanship
is just beautiful.

313
00:17:38,324 --> 00:17:40,258
Nice. It's a wedding quilt.

314
00:17:41,494 --> 00:17:43,905
Yeah...: How're you doing?

315
00:17:43,929 --> 00:17:45,074
Um, I'll meet you over

316
00:17:45,098 --> 00:17:47,464
at that cafe in a minute. Okay.

317
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:51,835
Yeah! Yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah...

318
00:17:51,871 --> 00:17:54,171
Folks coming home for Christmas.

319
00:17:54,207 --> 00:17:56,307
Folks coming home
for Thanksgiving.

320
00:17:56,342 --> 00:18:01,212
Just folks... coming home.

321
00:18:01,247 --> 00:18:03,547
Mister, you looking
for information?

322
00:18:03,582 --> 00:18:05,549
Going to cost you
a hundred bucks.

323
00:18:13,159 --> 00:18:15,719
Iced tea please, lots of lemon.

324
00:18:16,929 --> 00:18:18,562
Yeah, nothing like an iced tea

325
00:18:18,598 --> 00:18:20,442
on a hot day, is there? No.

326
00:18:20,466 --> 00:18:22,132
Say, you're from Atlanta?

327
00:18:22,167 --> 00:18:24,301
Uh, yes. How'd you know?

328
00:18:24,337 --> 00:18:26,002
Well, I'm a salesman.

329
00:18:26,038 --> 00:18:27,271
Farm implements.

330
00:18:27,306 --> 00:18:29,506
Good salesman
learns to read people.

331
00:18:29,542 --> 00:18:30,953
Oh, hey-hey-hey, no, no.

332
00:18:30,977 --> 00:18:32,621
This one's on me. Oh, no, no.

333
00:18:32,645 --> 00:18:34,356
No-no, look, look,
look... Hey, please.

334
00:18:34,380 --> 00:18:36,424
It's just my way of
saying, "Welcome to town."

335
00:18:36,448 --> 00:18:38,568
Well, thank you. Take care.

336
00:18:49,862 --> 00:18:52,063
Hey. I-I got lucky.

337
00:18:52,098 --> 00:18:53,497
Our mystery lady is not

338
00:18:53,532 --> 00:18:55,443
Sandra Daniels, the
name on the condo...

339
00:18:55,467 --> 00:18:56,601
Wanda Sutton.

340
00:18:56,636 --> 00:18:58,502
The lady with the quilts told me

341
00:18:58,538 --> 00:19:00,705
she saw Wanda
get off the noon bus.

342
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:03,440
She also told me
where Wanda lives,

343
00:19:03,476 --> 00:19:04,575
and that her boyfriend,

344
00:19:04,611 --> 00:19:07,211
Dan, lives way
back in the hills.

345
00:19:07,246 --> 00:19:10,881
Oh... Say, uh,
why don't you and I

346
00:19:10,917 --> 00:19:12,660
go sit in one of those
booths over there, huh?

347
00:19:12,684 --> 00:19:13,884
What do you say, sweet face?

348
00:19:13,920 --> 00:19:15,463
Yeah... Uh... Hey!

349
00:19:15,487 --> 00:19:16,932
Hey! Hey! Hey! Hey, listen!

350
00:19:16,956 --> 00:19:18,433
Don't start something
you can't finish, pops!

351
00:19:18,457 --> 00:19:19,601
Hey! "Pops"!

352
00:19:29,269 --> 00:19:31,202
Jackass!

353
00:19:32,338 --> 00:19:36,273
Touch your hair... "Pops"!

354
00:19:37,343 --> 00:19:39,076
You took care of him.

355
00:19:39,111 --> 00:19:41,245
Me, too. Thanks.

356
00:20:00,032 --> 00:20:03,634
Dang gone... Are we out of gas?

357
00:20:03,669 --> 00:20:05,168
No, we're not out of gas.

358
00:20:10,543 --> 00:20:12,254
Must be the carburetor.

359
00:20:12,278 --> 00:20:13,410
Oh, no.

360
00:20:13,446 --> 00:20:15,766
Oh, wait...

361
00:20:19,418 --> 00:20:21,085
I'll be right back.

362
00:20:23,189 --> 00:20:25,789
Well, you don't have
a distributor cap.

363
00:20:25,824 --> 00:20:27,591
Why not?

364
00:20:27,626 --> 00:20:28,959
Somebody took it.

365
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:31,028
You know where I can get one?

366
00:20:31,063 --> 00:20:33,030
Maybe.

367
00:20:33,065 --> 00:20:34,598
Going to cost you a hundred.

368
00:20:34,633 --> 00:20:37,067
Oh, come on! Ben, we can walk.

369
00:20:37,103 --> 00:20:39,281
The lady with the quilts
said that Wanda's place

370
00:20:39,305 --> 00:20:41,865
is just a mile or
two up the road.

371
00:20:48,581 --> 00:20:50,047
Yeah. Yeah.

372
00:20:50,082 --> 00:20:52,382
Hello! Hello!

373
00:20:52,418 --> 00:20:53,695
Hello!

374
00:20:53,719 --> 00:20:55,397
Hello!

375
00:20:55,421 --> 00:20:57,299
The name on the mailbox
says "Sutton," Yeah.

376
00:20:57,323 --> 00:20:59,167
So this must be the right
place. Wait, wait, wait...

377
00:20:59,191 --> 00:21:03,071
Oh! Good suit... Hello! Hello!

378
00:21:03,095 --> 00:21:04,662
Hey, here, here...

379
00:21:04,697 --> 00:21:07,097
Oh, great! Let's get
some of this business.

380
00:21:07,132 --> 00:21:09,266
Some of this business here.

381
00:21:09,301 --> 00:21:11,401
Ooh...

382
00:21:12,705 --> 00:21:17,319
Oh, thank you. Mmm... Oh, good?

383
00:21:17,343 --> 00:21:18,576
Oh, it's wonderful!

384
00:21:20,113 --> 00:21:21,712
Matlock!

385
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:29,987
Put your hands up.

386
00:21:41,067 --> 00:21:42,767
Thank you.

387
00:22:01,353 --> 00:22:08,225
Oh... Ben?

388
00:22:10,362 --> 00:22:12,296
Ben?

389
00:22:15,201 --> 00:22:17,234
Ben? Hmm?

390
00:22:17,269 --> 00:22:19,103
Were you asleep?

391
00:22:20,839 --> 00:22:23,084
I can't understand a
word you're saying.

392
00:22:23,108 --> 00:22:26,043
Ben, try to reach my ropes.

393
00:22:27,312 --> 00:22:30,047
Oh, who were those men?

394
00:22:30,082 --> 00:22:32,394
How did they know we
were looking for Wanda?

395
00:22:33,886 --> 00:22:35,597
Are they looking for Wanda, too?

396
00:22:37,557 --> 00:22:39,701
It doesn't make any sense.

397
00:22:39,725 --> 00:22:42,192
Why did they tie us up?

398
00:22:42,227 --> 00:22:44,205
I mean, why didn't
they just shoot us?

399
00:22:44,229 --> 00:22:45,707
I would have. Wouldn't you?

400
00:22:45,731 --> 00:22:47,164
No. No.

401
00:22:47,199 --> 00:22:50,500
Oh, I can't believe
this is happening to us.

402
00:22:50,536 --> 00:22:53,170
First, that long drive.

403
00:22:53,205 --> 00:22:56,173
And then, that awful
man in the cafe.

404
00:22:56,208 --> 00:22:58,353
And then, the distributor
cap and then we get tied up.

405
00:23:00,012 --> 00:23:01,156
Oh, excuse me.

406
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:03,080
My stomach is growling.

407
00:23:03,115 --> 00:23:05,661
We should have
eaten in that cafe.

408
00:23:07,687 --> 00:23:08,831
Weren't you hungry?

409
00:23:10,055 --> 00:23:12,590
You didn't say anything.

410
00:23:12,625 --> 00:23:14,169
Well, you should've
said something.

411
00:23:16,028 --> 00:23:18,340
Did you notice they had waffles?

412
00:23:18,364 --> 00:23:20,997
Pancakes. Waffles.

413
00:23:21,033 --> 00:23:25,502
Syrup... Waffles with apples...

414
00:23:25,538 --> 00:23:27,971
Waffles with cream cheese.

415
00:23:28,006 --> 00:23:29,846
Biscuits!

416
00:23:30,710 --> 00:23:32,209
Potato pie.

417
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:37,681
Scallop... Oh!

418
00:23:37,716 --> 00:23:39,150
Oh... I'm free.

419
00:23:40,353 --> 00:23:42,185
Put your hands up.

420
00:23:42,221 --> 00:23:43,554
Ben... Hmm...

421
00:23:43,589 --> 00:23:45,656
Uh, this is Ben
Matlock from Atlanta.

422
00:23:45,691 --> 00:23:46,890
He's a lawyer.

423
00:23:46,925 --> 00:23:49,070
This is my barn and
you're trespassing.

424
00:23:49,094 --> 00:23:51,729
Oh, are you Dan?

425
00:23:51,764 --> 00:23:53,197
How do you know my name?

426
00:23:53,232 --> 00:23:55,932
A... a lady from town

427
00:23:55,968 --> 00:23:58,502
said you were a good
friend of Wanda's.

428
00:23:58,538 --> 00:24:00,415
Wanda moved to Atlanta
a couple of years ago.

429
00:24:00,439 --> 00:24:01,772
Yes. Yes.

430
00:24:01,807 --> 00:24:03,040
That's why we're here.

431
00:24:03,075 --> 00:24:05,253
I told you to put
those hands up!

432
00:24:05,277 --> 00:24:07,222
I'm Julie March
and I'm a lawyer, too.

433
00:24:07,246 --> 00:24:09,546
Uh, actually,

434
00:24:09,581 --> 00:24:12,916
I'm with the
Atlanta D.A.'s office.

435
00:24:12,952 --> 00:24:16,754
Now, I know you're just
trying to protect Wanda,

436
00:24:16,789 --> 00:24:20,590
and that's wonderful, but
she's in terrible trouble.

437
00:24:20,626 --> 00:24:22,159
Can I untie him?

438
00:24:22,194 --> 00:24:26,497
Wanda is a witness to murder,

439
00:24:26,532 --> 00:24:28,966
and she can
identify the killers.

440
00:24:29,001 --> 00:24:31,267
But unfortunately,

441
00:24:31,303 --> 00:24:35,039
there are some very,
very dangerous men

442
00:24:35,074 --> 00:24:36,106
looking for her.

443
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:37,374
You ain't gonna hurt Wanda?

444
00:24:37,409 --> 00:24:39,577
No, no, we can see

445
00:24:39,612 --> 00:24:43,680
that she's protected. If
they don't get to her first.

446
00:24:43,715 --> 00:24:45,315
I got Wanda where she's safe.

447
00:24:45,351 --> 00:24:47,985
You can't hide her forever, Dan.

448
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:49,452
We want to help her.

449
00:25:30,429 --> 00:25:32,095
Wanda.

450
00:25:32,130 --> 00:25:34,532
It's me.

451
00:25:37,069 --> 00:25:39,036
You brought people.

452
00:25:39,071 --> 00:25:41,116
It's-it's Ben Matlock and
Julie March from court.

453
00:25:41,140 --> 00:25:42,420
We want to help.

454
00:25:44,810 --> 00:25:46,877
Thank you, Mr. Matlock.

455
00:25:46,912 --> 00:25:48,245
You've been very helpful.

456
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:50,192
Let's go. He's waiting.

457
00:25:50,216 --> 00:25:51,315
Wanda, no.

458
00:25:51,350 --> 00:25:53,016
Shut up!

459
00:25:53,052 --> 00:25:56,186
It's all right, Dan.
Please, they'll kill all of us.

460
00:25:56,221 --> 00:25:57,399
It's all right,
I'll go with you.

461
00:25:57,423 --> 00:25:58,667
Just please don't hurt them.

462
00:25:58,691 --> 00:26:00,624
Drop your gun!

463
00:26:00,659 --> 00:26:01,892
Don't even think of it!

464
00:26:01,927 --> 00:26:04,487
No!

465
00:26:10,836 --> 00:26:11,836
Stop!

466
00:26:17,709 --> 00:26:19,276
Come on, let's go.

467
00:26:27,753 --> 00:26:30,387
This is your fault, damn
it! You led those men here!

468
00:26:30,422 --> 00:26:32,222
Stop it! It's not his fault!

469
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:36,627
Who are those men?

470
00:26:36,662 --> 00:26:40,175
- They work for that guy.
- What guy?

471
00:26:40,199 --> 00:26:42,510
That foreigner who built the
big house down by the lake

472
00:26:42,534 --> 00:26:43,801
about three years ago.

473
00:26:43,836 --> 00:26:45,002
He ruined her life.

474
00:26:45,037 --> 00:26:48,372
One night, she just decided
to go to a party there.

475
00:26:48,407 --> 00:26:51,642
I told her they wouldn't let
her in, but she went anyway.

476
00:26:51,677 --> 00:26:53,610
The next day, she
moved to Atlanta.

477
00:26:55,013 --> 00:26:57,347
We were going to be married.

478
00:26:57,382 --> 00:27:00,483
Since I was eight years old,
I never loved anybody else.

479
00:27:09,662 --> 00:27:11,227
Where you going?

480
00:27:11,263 --> 00:27:12,474
I'm gonna circle around back.

481
00:27:12,498 --> 00:27:13,563
Be careful.

482
00:27:38,857 --> 00:27:42,192
Sorry, folks, the club's
closed for a private party.

483
00:27:42,227 --> 00:27:44,795
Um, my name's Ben
Matlock. I'm a lawyer.

484
00:27:44,830 --> 00:27:46,363
We need to see the host.

485
00:27:46,398 --> 00:27:49,566
Please turn your vehicle
around. You're on private property.

486
00:27:49,601 --> 00:27:51,435
I'm a deputy D.A.

487
00:27:51,470 --> 00:27:54,071
Now unless your boss wants
us back here in a half hour

488
00:27:54,106 --> 00:27:56,873
with a court order
and the police,

489
00:27:56,908 --> 00:27:58,875
you better let us in.

490
00:27:58,910 --> 00:28:00,377
Right through there.

491
00:28:11,090 --> 00:28:13,957
Is that our host?

492
00:28:19,031 --> 00:28:23,567
Uh..., Mr. Freemont?

493
00:28:23,602 --> 00:28:25,102
Well, Mr. Matlock,

494
00:28:25,137 --> 00:28:28,917
Ms. March. What a surprise
to see you, Mr. Freemont.

495
00:28:28,941 --> 00:28:32,609
My wife's family has a house
on the other side of the lake.

496
00:28:32,644 --> 00:28:34,077
We've summered here for years.

497
00:28:34,112 --> 00:28:35,546
Are you throwing this party?

498
00:28:35,581 --> 00:28:36,725
Oh, no, no, no.

499
00:28:36,749 --> 00:28:39,316
I'm just here to be neighborly.

500
00:28:39,351 --> 00:28:41,084
Why are you here?

501
00:28:41,119 --> 00:28:46,256
Uh, uh, well,
it's... a long story.

502
00:28:46,291 --> 00:28:47,690
Have a good time.

503
00:28:47,726 --> 00:28:48,758
Oh, thanks.

504
00:28:48,793 --> 00:28:52,662
Oh, excuse me. My wife. Okay.

505
00:28:52,697 --> 00:28:53,563
Curious, isn't it?

506
00:28:53,598 --> 00:28:54,764
I know.

507
00:28:58,036 --> 00:29:00,971
It's Albert Agura.

508
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:03,084
Excuse me. Oh, sure.

509
00:29:03,108 --> 00:29:04,341
Welcome.

510
00:29:04,376 --> 00:29:05,642
Good afternoon.

511
00:29:05,678 --> 00:29:07,377
Hello.

512
00:29:07,412 --> 00:29:09,479
Ladies.

513
00:29:09,514 --> 00:29:11,548
Look at this. Hello.

514
00:29:11,583 --> 00:29:13,361
I understand you wish to see me?

515
00:29:13,385 --> 00:29:15,052
Are you our host?

516
00:29:15,087 --> 00:29:16,620
That's my caviar.

517
00:29:16,655 --> 00:29:18,321
How can I help you?

518
00:29:18,356 --> 00:29:20,623
My name's Ben
Matlock. I'm a lawyer.

519
00:29:20,659 --> 00:29:21,792
This is Julie March.

520
00:29:21,827 --> 00:29:23,894
She's the assistant
D.A. from Atlanta.

521
00:29:23,929 --> 00:29:26,596
We're looking for a
material witness to a murder

522
00:29:26,631 --> 00:29:28,432
and we have reason to believe

523
00:29:28,467 --> 00:29:30,300
she was brought
here against her will.

524
00:29:30,335 --> 00:29:33,003
Forced to come to my party?

525
00:29:33,038 --> 00:29:35,305
And who is this rotten guest?

526
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:36,740
Wanda Sutton.

527
00:29:36,776 --> 00:29:38,253
Do you know her, Mr. Agura?

528
00:29:38,277 --> 00:29:42,312
Everybody that lives by the
lake knows Wanda Sutton.

529
00:29:42,348 --> 00:29:45,649
Robert, have you
seen Ms. Sutton?

530
00:29:50,355 --> 00:29:52,255
In the boathouse.

531
00:29:52,291 --> 00:29:54,091
One of her favorite spots.

532
00:29:54,126 --> 00:29:57,393
It's quite possible she would
not want to be disturbed.

533
00:29:59,597 --> 00:30:02,532
<i>Now I get it.</i>

534
00:30:02,567 --> 00:30:05,268
Agura's that foreigner
Wanda ran off with.

535
00:30:05,304 --> 00:30:07,604
Yeah, yeah.

536
00:30:07,639 --> 00:30:11,308
Millions, millions,
millions in drugs. Ruthless.

537
00:30:11,343 --> 00:30:13,443
Wanda is his mistress

538
00:30:13,479 --> 00:30:15,457
and he wanted his mistress back.

539
00:30:15,481 --> 00:30:18,915
Yeah. Yeah, I guess
the Justice Department

540
00:30:18,950 --> 00:30:21,418
would like to talk to Wanda
after all she's seen and heard.

541
00:30:21,453 --> 00:30:23,520
Oh, that poor girl.

542
00:30:23,555 --> 00:30:26,689
I bet Wanda saw
something just recently

543
00:30:26,725 --> 00:30:30,060
that Mr. Agura just as
soon she not talk about.

544
00:31:20,446 --> 00:31:22,379
Right there.

545
00:31:23,982 --> 00:31:26,617
Bring it up a little closer.

546
00:31:32,424 --> 00:31:34,291
Not a sign of them.

547
00:31:34,326 --> 00:31:36,660
She can't have
disappeared into thin air.

548
00:31:38,364 --> 00:31:40,664
Oh, thank you so much.

549
00:31:40,699 --> 00:31:41,531
Keep searching.

550
00:31:41,566 --> 00:31:43,566
Whatever it takes.

551
00:31:43,602 --> 00:31:45,013
I feel terrible that
something like this

552
00:31:45,037 --> 00:31:47,282
could happen to a guest of mine.

553
00:31:47,306 --> 00:31:48,505
Yes, sir.

554
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:50,840
Excuse me.

555
00:31:50,876 --> 00:31:53,977
Did I see you in town?

556
00:31:54,012 --> 00:31:56,045
Yep.

557
00:31:56,081 --> 00:31:58,047
At the store.

558
00:31:58,083 --> 00:32:00,183
I do chores for Mr. Agura.

559
00:32:01,787 --> 00:32:03,597
<i>Mr. Matlock, I want to help,</i>

560
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:05,789
but I gave you everything
David was working on

561
00:32:05,824 --> 00:32:08,157
when you first came in
here right after he was shot.

562
00:32:08,193 --> 00:32:10,861
I know you have, and I
appreciate it, I really do,

563
00:32:10,896 --> 00:32:12,428
but humor me.

564
00:32:12,464 --> 00:32:14,642
Run just one more computer check

565
00:32:14,666 --> 00:32:17,011
to see where it leads,
what do you say?

566
00:32:17,035 --> 00:32:18,902
All right, I'll give it a try.

567
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:30,124
Ben, Ben, are you in there?

568
00:32:30,148 --> 00:32:32,348
Yeah. Yeah.

569
00:32:32,383 --> 00:32:35,518
I'm just... I'm
just sitting here.

570
00:32:36,722 --> 00:32:38,622
Hi.

571
00:32:38,657 --> 00:32:39,723
Hi.

572
00:32:39,758 --> 00:32:43,426
You know, I've been thinking.

573
00:32:43,462 --> 00:32:45,128
About the case?

574
00:32:45,164 --> 00:32:47,730
Yeah. I wish we could
have found Wanda.

575
00:32:47,766 --> 00:32:49,599
Yeah.

576
00:32:49,635 --> 00:32:52,802
Do you think we should
ask for a postponement?

577
00:32:52,837 --> 00:33:00,837
Um... um... Uh, no, I think...

578
00:33:02,013 --> 00:33:05,182
I think I'd like to go ahead.

579
00:33:05,217 --> 00:33:07,495
Why, have you
found out something?

580
00:33:07,519 --> 00:33:09,786
Well, I might have.

581
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:13,990
Well, you certainly have
a lot of opportunities.

582
00:33:14,025 --> 00:33:17,427
Your client certainly
could have done it.

583
00:33:17,462 --> 00:33:19,507
I mean, her husband
was having an affair

584
00:33:19,531 --> 00:33:21,276
and the gun was in her hand.

585
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:22,465
Yeah, the gun...

586
00:33:22,501 --> 00:33:26,403
Or it could have been
either of those two guys

587
00:33:26,438 --> 00:33:28,838
who worked for Agura. Yeah.

588
00:33:28,873 --> 00:33:30,674
Or Agura himself.

589
00:33:30,709 --> 00:33:31,986
He certainly had motive.

590
00:33:32,010 --> 00:33:33,954
I mean, his girlfriend
was cheating on him

591
00:33:33,978 --> 00:33:35,445
with Laura's husband.

592
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:37,280
But don't get me wrong...

593
00:33:37,316 --> 00:33:41,050
I still believe Laura
McCord is guilty.

594
00:33:43,488 --> 00:33:50,527
Um... You know, uh,
you know, when I, uh...

595
00:33:50,562 --> 00:33:53,796
uncover something, you know,

596
00:33:53,832 --> 00:33:55,532
that might be important...

597
00:33:55,567 --> 00:33:58,535
A clue or some
evidence or something...

598
00:33:58,570 --> 00:34:01,904
I, uh, the first
thing I think is, um,

599
00:34:01,940 --> 00:34:03,406
I want to tell you.

600
00:34:03,442 --> 00:34:06,375
It is?

601
00:34:06,411 --> 00:34:07,743
Sometimes.

602
00:34:07,779 --> 00:34:10,480
Oh, but you can't do that. No.

603
00:34:10,516 --> 00:34:12,649
No, no. Ethics and all.

604
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:21,691
It's a strange life
we lead, isn't it?

605
00:34:21,726 --> 00:34:24,194
Sometimes we're together,

606
00:34:24,229 --> 00:34:27,597
and sometimes we're poles apart.

607
00:34:27,632 --> 00:34:29,199
I know.

608
00:34:29,234 --> 00:34:33,236
But we... almost got a chance

609
00:34:33,271 --> 00:34:35,405
to work together this
one time, didn't we?

610
00:34:35,441 --> 00:34:39,542
Yeah, trudging up those hills.

611
00:34:39,577 --> 00:34:44,848
Oh, I have never been
so hungry and so dusty.

612
00:34:44,883 --> 00:34:46,082
And tired.

613
00:34:46,117 --> 00:34:47,217
Lost.

614
00:34:47,252 --> 00:34:48,985
And footsore.

615
00:34:49,020 --> 00:34:51,655
I had the time of my life.

616
00:34:51,690 --> 00:34:54,457
I'll certainly never forget it.

617
00:34:54,492 --> 00:34:55,926
Nor I.

618
00:34:55,961 --> 00:34:58,394
Yeah?

619
00:35:01,266 --> 00:35:04,367
Whichever way this
turns out, I wish...

620
00:35:04,403 --> 00:35:06,136
I do, too.

621
00:35:06,171 --> 00:35:08,204
Do you swear to tell
the truth, the whole truth

622
00:35:08,239 --> 00:35:10,118
and nothing but the truth? I do.

623
00:35:10,142 --> 00:35:13,443
Once again, you're a hell
of a driving companion.

624
00:35:13,478 --> 00:35:17,647
Let's go somewhere
sometime just for fun.

625
00:35:17,683 --> 00:35:19,960
Yeah, let's do, let's do. And...

626
00:35:19,984 --> 00:35:22,018
If I might interrupt.

627
00:35:22,053 --> 00:35:24,353
Are you prepared to
examine this witness?

628
00:35:24,389 --> 00:35:26,255
Uh, yes, ma'am.

629
00:35:28,393 --> 00:35:35,031
Uh, Mr. Agura, that's
an elegant home

630
00:35:35,067 --> 00:35:38,869
you have out near
Gunther's Creek.

631
00:35:38,904 --> 00:35:40,003
We like it.

632
00:35:40,038 --> 00:35:42,005
It's nice.

633
00:35:42,040 --> 00:35:44,674
And that must mean
business is good?

634
00:35:44,710 --> 00:35:46,309
Indeed.

635
00:35:46,344 --> 00:35:48,845
Exactly what
business are you in?

636
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:52,582
I deal in international
finance. Oh.

637
00:35:52,617 --> 00:35:58,188
So you're a... an important
man, a powerful man.

638
00:35:58,223 --> 00:36:00,790
I wouldn't say that.

639
00:36:00,825 --> 00:36:03,326
Isn't it true though,
that you travel

640
00:36:03,361 --> 00:36:05,461
in the company of
two bodyguards...

641
00:36:05,497 --> 00:36:07,931
Eduardo Cortez and Frank Grady?

642
00:36:07,966 --> 00:36:11,434
I employ literally
hundreds of people.

643
00:36:11,469 --> 00:36:14,604
Including, uh, Eduardo
Cortez and Frank Grady.

644
00:36:14,639 --> 00:36:19,042
Yes. Uh...

645
00:36:19,077 --> 00:36:23,145
Uh, this is, uh,
People's Exhibit G.

646
00:36:23,181 --> 00:36:25,815
This gun is owned
by the defendant.

647
00:36:25,851 --> 00:36:27,517
Do you recognize
this gun, do you?

648
00:36:27,552 --> 00:36:28,752
Not at all.

649
00:36:28,787 --> 00:36:30,587
If, uh...

650
00:36:30,622 --> 00:36:33,990
If I told you that
this gun was last

651
00:36:34,025 --> 00:36:38,761
in the possession of your
employee, uh, Mr. Grady,

652
00:36:38,797 --> 00:36:41,031
would that stir
your recollection?

653
00:36:41,066 --> 00:36:42,365
No.

654
00:36:45,203 --> 00:36:48,838
Isn't it true... that you were

655
00:36:48,873 --> 00:36:52,509
at the scene of David
McCord's murder?

656
00:36:54,046 --> 00:36:56,346
No, it is not true. And during

657
00:36:56,381 --> 00:37:00,850
that scene, uh, the
defendant... The victim's wife,

658
00:37:00,885 --> 00:37:03,853
uh, arrived, and collapsed.

659
00:37:03,888 --> 00:37:09,792
And at that time, you
removed this gun from her hand

660
00:37:09,828 --> 00:37:12,395
and placed the murder
weapon in her hand.

661
00:37:12,430 --> 00:37:15,064
And even though the
victim was already dead,

662
00:37:15,099 --> 00:37:18,902
you fired a second
shot into his body.

663
00:37:18,937 --> 00:37:21,136
Leaving powder marks on her hand

664
00:37:21,172 --> 00:37:23,740
to make it look as if
she were the real killer.

665
00:37:23,775 --> 00:37:25,442
I did no such thing.

666
00:37:25,477 --> 00:37:28,578
And then, you instructed your
employee to get rid of this gun.

667
00:37:28,613 --> 00:37:32,048
And unbeknownst
to you, he kept it.

668
00:37:32,083 --> 00:37:35,619
Now... before you answer,

669
00:37:35,654 --> 00:37:38,321
I have to warn you.

670
00:37:38,356 --> 00:37:42,859
Mr. Grady had agreed
to testify to these facts.

671
00:37:45,363 --> 00:37:46,829
All right.

672
00:37:46,865 --> 00:37:48,330
It's true.

673
00:37:48,366 --> 00:37:50,867
But I did not kill David McCord!

674
00:37:53,371 --> 00:37:54,671
No further questions.

675
00:37:58,709 --> 00:37:59,909
Why did you stop?

676
00:37:59,944 --> 00:38:01,478
He didn't do it.

677
00:38:02,981 --> 00:38:06,316
Mr. Matlock, would
you like to reconsider?

678
00:38:06,351 --> 00:38:07,628
Uh, no, ma'am.

679
00:38:07,652 --> 00:38:09,797
Uh, Defense would like to call

680
00:38:09,821 --> 00:38:12,381
Mr. Thomas
Freemont to the stand.

681
00:38:13,191 --> 00:38:14,991
Mr. Freemont, you're president

682
00:38:15,027 --> 00:38:17,527
of the Atlanta Crestmore Bank.

683
00:38:17,562 --> 00:38:18,728
Isn't that right?

684
00:38:18,764 --> 00:38:22,498
I am, sir, for almost...
five years now.

685
00:38:22,534 --> 00:38:25,235
Yeah. And-and
w-w-what kind of shape

686
00:38:25,270 --> 00:38:27,703
was the, the bank in
when you took it over?

687
00:38:27,739 --> 00:38:31,241
Oh, it was a very solid,
medium-sized bank.

688
00:38:31,276 --> 00:38:33,810
But you really turned
it around, didn't you?

689
00:38:33,845 --> 00:38:36,980
Well... we have experienced
some growth, yes.

690
00:38:38,516 --> 00:38:40,649
You're being very modest.

691
00:38:40,685 --> 00:38:43,486
Your bank is one of the five

692
00:38:43,521 --> 00:38:45,588
largest banks in
the city, isn't it?

693
00:38:45,623 --> 00:38:47,823
One of the five
largest in the state,

694
00:38:47,859 --> 00:38:49,426
as long as I'm not being modest.

695
00:38:49,461 --> 00:38:51,927
Yeah.

696
00:38:51,963 --> 00:38:54,664
Uh, how did you come to know,

697
00:38:54,699 --> 00:38:57,333
uh, our missing
witness, Wanda Sutton?

698
00:38:57,368 --> 00:38:58,501
Well, I...

699
00:38:58,536 --> 00:39:00,904
I'm afraid I don't
know her at all.

700
00:39:00,939 --> 00:39:02,972
Well, I...

701
00:39:03,007 --> 00:39:05,908
I... well, now, I
am confused. Um...

702
00:39:07,646 --> 00:39:12,948
According to these,
uh, computer... uh, runs,

703
00:39:12,984 --> 00:39:14,617
you do know her.

704
00:39:14,653 --> 00:39:16,397
Mr. Matlock, the witness

705
00:39:16,421 --> 00:39:18,754
has already answered
your question.

706
00:39:18,790 --> 00:39:20,122
Oh, I apologize, Your Honor.

707
00:39:20,158 --> 00:39:21,358
I'll rephrase.

708
00:39:21,393 --> 00:39:24,194
Uh, I guess you could
look at, uh, these runs

709
00:39:24,229 --> 00:39:27,630
and could explain them to us.

710
00:39:27,666 --> 00:39:30,199
Oh, yes. These are
some of the computerized

711
00:39:30,234 --> 00:39:33,002
accounting statements for
the bank's promotion account.

712
00:39:33,037 --> 00:39:34,537
Now, there is a...

713
00:39:34,573 --> 00:39:39,642
there is a bill that comes up
month after month after month

714
00:39:39,678 --> 00:39:44,380
in the amount of $3,280.

715
00:39:44,416 --> 00:39:48,250
And it goes right into your
residential mortgage account.

716
00:39:48,286 --> 00:39:49,819
Doesn't it?

717
00:39:49,854 --> 00:39:55,224
Well, I see it, but I
don't know what it's for.

718
00:39:55,259 --> 00:39:57,961
Those are the mortgage payments

719
00:39:57,996 --> 00:40:00,630
on Wanda Sutton's condominium.

720
00:40:02,366 --> 00:40:04,701
- Authorized by you.
- Your Honor?

721
00:40:04,736 --> 00:40:08,104
Is Mr. Matlock sure
where he's going with this?

722
00:40:08,139 --> 00:40:09,406
Mr. Matlock?

723
00:40:09,441 --> 00:40:12,975
Uh... Yes.

724
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:16,056
And here is

725
00:40:16,080 --> 00:40:18,514
something that's
even more fascinating.

726
00:40:18,550 --> 00:40:21,851
Uh, are you aware...

727
00:40:21,886 --> 00:40:27,190
that Wanda Sutton has a, uh,
credit card issued by your bank?

728
00:40:27,225 --> 00:40:30,627
Well, the bank issues
thousands of credit cards.

729
00:40:30,662 --> 00:40:32,507
Well, this isn't an
ordinary credit card.

730
00:40:32,531 --> 00:40:36,365
This is one of those
fancy platinum credit cards.

731
00:40:37,735 --> 00:40:40,503
With no credit limits.

732
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:44,206
No credit limit...

733
00:40:44,242 --> 00:40:47,176
For a girl with no
apparent means of support.

734
00:40:48,413 --> 00:40:51,314
And you approved...

735
00:40:51,349 --> 00:40:53,182
her application, didn't you?

736
00:40:54,686 --> 00:41:01,057
And arranged that the
bills be paid from the fund.

737
00:41:03,694 --> 00:41:05,328
Well, maybe we better

738
00:41:05,363 --> 00:41:08,064
examine these
figures. No, no, no.

739
00:41:08,099 --> 00:41:10,633
That's not necessary.

740
00:41:10,668 --> 00:41:12,502
I issued the card.

741
00:41:12,537 --> 00:41:14,804
And the bills were
paid from the fund?

742
00:41:14,839 --> 00:41:16,706
If you say so.

743
00:41:16,741 --> 00:41:19,008
Hmm...

744
00:41:19,044 --> 00:41:23,146
And David McCord worked
in the credit card department.

745
00:41:25,884 --> 00:41:27,984
And that popped
up in his computer.

746
00:41:28,019 --> 00:41:30,086
And he went over
to the condominium

747
00:41:30,121 --> 00:41:31,198
you shared with your mistress.

748
00:41:31,222 --> 00:41:32,433
Confronted you.
Maybe he was going

749
00:41:32,457 --> 00:41:33,667
to blackmail
you... I don't know.

750
00:41:33,691 --> 00:41:34,835
But that's why you killed him.

751
00:41:34,859 --> 00:41:36,139
Now, wait a minute.

752
00:41:37,795 --> 00:41:39,929
All right, Wanda
and I were lovers.

753
00:41:39,965 --> 00:41:42,598
And perhaps, I did use funds

754
00:41:42,633 --> 00:41:45,201
from the bank's
promotion account.

755
00:41:45,237 --> 00:41:47,703
But that's a far cry
from killing anybody.

756
00:41:52,077 --> 00:41:54,043
Yeah.

757
00:41:54,079 --> 00:41:56,212
Yeah, maybe you're right.

758
00:41:56,247 --> 00:41:58,047
But there is one other thing.

759
00:41:58,083 --> 00:41:59,415
There is one other thing.

760
00:41:59,450 --> 00:42:04,320
About a year ago, your big...

761
00:42:04,355 --> 00:42:08,424
important bank was in
serious financial trouble.

762
00:42:08,459 --> 00:42:12,161
You expanded too fast.

763
00:42:12,196 --> 00:42:17,600
Several big loans went sour
and you needed cash bad.

764
00:42:17,635 --> 00:42:20,236
The bank is one of the
strongest in the South.

765
00:42:20,271 --> 00:42:22,705
Only because you made
your deal with the devil,

766
00:42:22,740 --> 00:42:23,806
and you got a windfall.

767
00:42:23,841 --> 00:42:27,276
Huge cash deposits.

768
00:42:27,311 --> 00:42:29,678
Vast amounts of money.

769
00:42:29,714 --> 00:42:36,686
$100 million in drug money
from your friend, Mr. Agura.

770
00:42:36,721 --> 00:42:38,254
And then suddenly,

771
00:42:38,289 --> 00:42:40,489
you were only his employee.

772
00:42:41,726 --> 00:42:43,092
His slave.

773
00:42:43,127 --> 00:42:46,362
You were forced to
launder drug money

774
00:42:46,397 --> 00:42:49,365
month after month
after month after month.

775
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:55,337
And that's what popped up
in David McCord's computer.

776
00:42:56,741 --> 00:42:59,542
And that is a federal offense

777
00:42:59,577 --> 00:43:02,211
with years in prison.

778
00:43:02,246 --> 00:43:05,314
And that<i> is</i> motive for murder.

779
00:43:05,350 --> 00:43:06,316
Isn't it?

780
00:43:06,351 --> 00:43:10,453
I did not kill David McCord!

781
00:43:11,856 --> 00:43:13,901
I wasn't even there
the night of the murder!

782
00:43:13,925 --> 00:43:15,925
Yes, you were.

783
00:43:15,960 --> 00:43:17,860
I was not!

784
00:43:18,929 --> 00:43:20,229
Where do you buy your suits?

785
00:43:23,034 --> 00:43:25,000
Well, my...

786
00:43:25,036 --> 00:43:27,670
They're custom-made
for me by my tailor.

787
00:43:27,705 --> 00:43:30,439
I imagine they're
expensive. Yes, yes, they are.

788
00:43:30,475 --> 00:43:32,753
What do you do with them when
you're through wearing them?

789
00:43:32,777 --> 00:43:33,943
I give them to charity.

790
00:43:33,978 --> 00:43:34,944
All of them?

791
00:43:34,979 --> 00:43:35,979
Yes.

792
00:43:37,048 --> 00:43:40,149
All... but one.

793
00:43:41,719 --> 00:43:43,686
See, when...

794
00:43:43,721 --> 00:43:46,288
When I went back up
to that condominium

795
00:43:46,324 --> 00:43:49,125
looking for clues, I had to...

796
00:43:49,160 --> 00:43:51,760
I had to go up the stairs.

797
00:43:53,064 --> 00:43:57,199
And I rubbed against
the wall, and I got...

798
00:43:57,235 --> 00:44:00,236
red paint on my coat.

799
00:44:01,606 --> 00:44:03,317
They've been painting
that building for months.

800
00:44:03,341 --> 00:44:06,041
They've been painting it
for three and a half years,

801
00:44:06,077 --> 00:44:07,376
and they're still not finished.

802
00:44:07,411 --> 00:44:08,610
Now, I imagine

803
00:44:08,646 --> 00:44:11,814
when you went up and
down that penthouse,

804
00:44:11,849 --> 00:44:13,149
you used the elevator.

805
00:44:13,184 --> 00:44:15,284
But on the night
of the murder...

806
00:44:17,855 --> 00:44:19,555
you used the stairs.

807
00:44:20,658 --> 00:44:23,626
You used the stairs
to avoid the police.

808
00:44:25,029 --> 00:44:28,564
I was not in the apartment!

809
00:44:30,435 --> 00:44:33,702
And you rubbed against
the wall just like I did.

810
00:44:33,737 --> 00:44:36,738
You got red paint on your suit.

811
00:44:36,774 --> 00:44:39,775
And when you got home
to your house on the lake,

812
00:44:39,810 --> 00:44:42,344
you threw that suit away.

813
00:44:42,380 --> 00:44:44,646
Now if I threw my suit away,

814
00:44:44,682 --> 00:44:46,722
I don't imagine
anybody would pick it up.

815
00:44:46,750 --> 00:44:49,118
Of course, I wouldn't
throw it away.

816
00:44:49,153 --> 00:44:53,623
But somebody found your suit.

817
00:44:53,658 --> 00:44:58,995
An expensive pinstripe suit.

818
00:44:59,030 --> 00:45:02,298
What's a little paint?

819
00:45:02,333 --> 00:45:06,001
And that person
has been very proud

820
00:45:06,036 --> 00:45:07,670
to wear that suit.

821
00:45:11,375 --> 00:45:14,176
Yes, sir.

822
00:45:14,212 --> 00:45:16,145
Yes, sir, right here.

823
00:45:16,181 --> 00:45:18,080
Just come right
here, right here.

824
00:45:18,116 --> 00:45:19,549
Here.

825
00:45:19,584 --> 00:45:21,751
See right here?

826
00:45:26,725 --> 00:45:28,591
Red paint.

827
00:45:30,928 --> 00:45:33,763
Same shade.

828
00:45:33,798 --> 00:45:37,967
This coat even has your initials

829
00:45:38,002 --> 00:45:40,269
on the inside pocket.

830
00:45:40,304 --> 00:45:42,671
That's your coat.

831
00:45:47,178 --> 00:45:50,680
I am not answering
any more questions.

832
00:45:50,715 --> 00:45:55,717
I think that's a sound move.

833
00:45:55,753 --> 00:45:58,354
Your Honor, at this time
I request that all charges

834
00:45:58,389 --> 00:46:01,190
against my client be dropped.

835
00:46:01,225 --> 00:46:02,825
The People concur, Your Honor.

836
00:46:02,860 --> 00:46:04,004
Case dismissed.

837
00:46:08,499 --> 00:46:11,767
♪ ♪

838
00:46:21,812 --> 00:46:24,213
Oh, here we are.

839
00:46:24,249 --> 00:46:25,648
Ice cold.

840
00:46:25,683 --> 00:46:28,617
How did you get
such an inspired idea?

841
00:46:28,653 --> 00:46:30,519
Oh, it must be the
company I keep.

842
00:46:40,831 --> 00:46:43,165
We've been looking
all over for you.

843
00:46:43,201 --> 00:46:44,467
Hi.

844
00:46:44,502 --> 00:46:46,702
If you want to
testify, the trial's over.

845
00:46:46,737 --> 00:46:48,715
Actually, we heard you were
down here having a picnic,

846
00:46:48,739 --> 00:46:50,184
so we brought you
some blueberry cobbler.

847
00:46:50,208 --> 00:46:51,785
Oh! Thank you. And some news.

848
00:46:51,809 --> 00:46:55,289
- We got married.
- Oh, Danny!

849
00:46:55,313 --> 00:46:58,614
Hey, you know, you said
let's go somewhere for fun.

850
00:46:58,650 --> 00:47:00,449
This turned out to be perfect.

851
00:47:00,484 --> 00:47:02,529
Let's help ourselves.
Chicken looks mighty good.

852
00:47:02,553 --> 00:47:04,198
Yes, oh, yes, help yourself.

853
00:47:04,222 --> 00:47:07,623
Let me try some of that
blueberry cobbler. There you go.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
